DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The kkeerrnn__rreebboooott() function handles final system shutdown, and either
     halts, reboots, or powers down the system.  The exact action to be taken
     is determined by the flags passed in _h_o_w_t_o.

     The relevant flags are:
           RB_HALT        Halt the system in-place rather than restarting.
           RB_POWEROFF    Power down the system rather than restarting.
           RB_POWERCYCLE  Request a power-cycle in addition to restarting.
           RB_NOSYNC      Do not sync filesystems during shutdown.
           RB_DUMP        Dump kernel memory during shutdown.

     The _h_o_w_t_o field, and its full list of flags are described in additional
     detail by reboot(2).

     kkeerrnn__rreebboooott() performs the following actions:

           1.   Set the _r_e_b_o_o_t_i_n_g variable to 1, indicating that the reboot
                process has begun and cannot be stopped.

           2.   Unless the RB_NOSYNC flag is set in _h_o_w_t_o, sync and unmount
                the system's disks by calling vfs_unmountall(9).

           3.   If rebooting after a panic (RB_DUMP is set in _h_o_w_t_o, but
                RB_HALT is not set), initiate a system crash dump via
                ddooaadduummpp().

           4.   Print a message indicating that the system is about to be
                halted or rebooted, and a report of the total system uptime.

           5.   Execute all registered shutdown hooks.  See _S_H_U_T_D_O_W_N _H_O_O_K_S be‐
                low.

           6.   As a last resort, if none of the shutdown hooks handled the
                reboot, call the machine-dependent ccppuu__rreesseett() function.  In
                the unlikely case that this is not supported, kkeerrnn__rreebboooott()
                will loop forever at the end of the function.  This requires a
                manual reset of the system.

     kkeerrnn__rreebboooott() may be called from a typical kernel execution context, when
     the system is running normally.  It may also be called as the final step
     of a kernel panic, or from the kernel debugger.  Therefore, the code in
     this function is subject to restrictions described by the _E_X_E_C_U_T_I_O_N
     _C_O_N_T_E_X_T section of the panic(9) man page.

     The sshhuuttddoowwnn__nniiccee() function is the intended path for performing a clean
     reboot or shutdown when the system is operating under normal conditions.
     Calling this function will send a signal to the init(8) process, in‐
     structing it to perform a shutdown.  When init(8) has cleanly terminated
     its children, it will perform the reboot(2) system call, which in turn
     calls kkeerrnn__rreebboooott().

     If sshhuuttddoowwnn__nniiccee() is called before the init(8) process has been spawned,
     or if the system has panicked or otherwise halted, kkeerrnn__rreebboooott() will be
     called directly.

SSHHUUTTDDOOWWNN HHOOOOKKSS
     The system defines three separate EVENTHANDLER(9) events, which are in‐
     voked successively during the shutdown procedure.  These are
     _s_h_u_t_d_o_w_n___p_r_e___s_y_n_c, _s_h_u_t_d_o_w_n___p_o_s_t___s_y_n_c, and _s_h_u_t_d_o_w_n___f_i_n_a_l.  They will be
     executed unconditionally in the listed order.  Handler functions regis‐
     tered to any of these events will receive the value of _h_o_w_t_o as their
     second argument, which may be used to decide what action to take.

     The _s_h_u_t_d_o_w_n___p_r_e___s_y_n_c event is invoked before syncing filesystems to
     disk.  It enables any action or state transition that must happen before
     this point to take place.

     The _s_h_u_t_d_o_w_n___p_o_s_t___s_y_n_c event is invoked at the point immediately after
     the filesystem sync has finished.  It enables, for example, disk drivers
     to complete the sync by flushing their cache to disk.  Note that this
     event still takes place before the optional kernel core dump.

     The _s_h_u_t_d_o_w_n___f_i_n_a_l event is invoked as the very last step of
     kkeerrnn__rreebboooott().  Drivers and subsystems such as acpi(4) can register han‐
     dlers to this event that will perform the actual reboot, power-off, or
     halt.

     Notably, the _s_h_u_t_d_o_w_n___f_i_n_a_l event is also the point at which all kernel
     modules will have their shutdown (MOD_SHUTDOWN) hooks executed, and when
     the DEVICE_SHUTDOWN(9) method will be executed recursively on all de‐
     vices.

     All event handlers, like kkeerrnn__rreebboooott() itself, may be run in either nor‐
     mal shutdown context or a kernel panic or debugger context.  Handler
     functions are expected to take care not to trigger recursive panics.

